
To track logs in our application, we use splunk.

    Benefits of using splunk
    -----------------------------
        1. Offers enhanced GUI and real-time visibility in a dashboard.
        2. It reduces troubleshooting and resolving time by offering instant results.
        3. It is the best suited-tool for root cause analysis.
        4. Splunk allows you to generate graphs, alerts, and dashboards.
        5. You can easily search and investigate specific results using splunk.
        6. It allows you to troubleshoot any condition of failure for improved performance.

To use splunk

    ->  download splunk from splunk.com by adding some general personal details. and then install and open.
    ->  On splunk dashboard, on top right toggle menu,
    ->  click on setting option -> Data Inputs -> HTTP event collector
    ->  In Http event collector page, click on newToken btn, and the create token page will be visible.
    ->  then here, provide token name, sourceName, description, and check enable indexer acknowledgement checkbox.
    ->  now create index on same page next step (if we have multiple env's then we can create multiple indexes for all respective env's)
    ->  and after creating index, choose that index then select source type (source type is our logging tools that we are going to use like log4j)
    ->  inside same page in third step, we can review all the details which we feeded.
    ->  then w will get many thing on review step like, name, source name override, sourceType, index. (with these all info we will create xml file
        into our spring boot application).
    ->  then after review submit that generate token form. and we will get msg "Token has been created successfully,"

        Create spring boot CRUD project
        ===============================

    ->  if we are using log4j, then exclude spring boot by default starter logging. by adding exclusion dependency inside pom.xml file.
    ->  add log4j2 dependency.
    ->  add logs inside your business logic, which we want to track.
    ->  create xml file inside resources folder of our spring bot CRUD app,
    ->  on dashboard click on search and reporting option,
    ->  search logs on search box by providing index-name and then you will get all logs

        -----------------------------------------------------------------
        index="spring_dev", also we can set time like 5 mins, 30 mins etc
        -----------------------------------------------------------------


                XML file code
                =============

                <?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
                <Configuration>
                    <Appenders>
                        <Console name="console" target="SYSTEM_OUT">
                            <PatternLayout
                                    pattern="%style{%d{ISO8601}} %highlight{%-5level }[%style{%t}{bright,blue}] %style{%C{10}}{bright,yellow}: %msg%n%throwable" />
                        </Console>
                        <SplunkHttp
                                name="splunk-http"
                                url="http://localhost:8088"
                                token="55264696-1a4d-4e39-a9dd-642272a08f0d"
                                host="localhost"
                                index="spring_dev"
                                type="raw"
                                source="source name"
                                source-type="log4j"
                                messageFormat="text"
                                disableCertificateValidation="true">
                            <PatternLayout pattern="%m" />
                        </SplunkHttp>

                    </Appenders>

                    <Loggers>
                        <Root level="info">
                            <AppenderRef ref="console" />
                            <AppenderRef ref="splunkhttp" />
                        </Root>
                    </Loggers>
                </Configuration>



======================================== Splunk Alerting ============================================

    setting -> server setting -> in server setting page -> click on email settings
        -> the provide host, username, password, domains, etc. then click save btn
        -> add custom condition like suppose any api failing and we want alert on email then this status we can set as condition.
        -> on dashboard, suppose we searched a log then right top we will get save as btn and click on alert then alert model will open.
           inside that we have to set condition, suppose if any error then we want alert on email.
        -> alert option is also there on dashboard, where we can check how many alerts we have created